subset (a mechanically-generated Zig vectors file, not the upstream JSON
file itself) is within what the licence grants, same as every other
`modules/<name>/NOTICE` "third-party attribution" file in this repository.

No third-party SOURCE CODE is present anywhere in this module — only test
vectors. `bolt1.zig`/`bolt2.zig`/`bolt7.zig` are clean-room from the public
BOLT#1/#2/#7 specifications (see README.md's "Provenance:" line and
SPEC.md); no third-party Lightning implementation's CODE was ported,
studied, or consulted — the `lightningdevkit/rust-lightning` vectors below
are frozen HEX OUTPUT their tests assert against, extracted mechanically
without reading how their encoder/decoder is implemented (see "What an
anchor is" reasoning in the second source's section below).

## `lightning/bolts` — the BOLT#7 specification repository's own
## test-vector suite

  https://github.com/lightning/bolts
  Licensed under CC-BY 4.0: http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/
  (stated in the repository's own README.md; there is no separate LICENSE
  file — CC-BY 4.0 has none to reproduce beyond the attribution above).
  Fetched 2026-08-02 at commit `35e79db504560b9d3494a0ed07bf1e8379c3663a`
  (branch `master`) — the same commit already cited in the sibling
  `modules/lninvoice/NOTICE`.

One file is a test-vector source for this module:

  * `bolt07/extended-queries.json` (10 rows, ALL vendored verbatim) —
    transcribed mechanically (never hand-typed) into
    `src/bolt7_extended_queries_kat_vectors.zig`; each row's `hex` wire
    bytes and the `msg` fields this module's codec actually interprets
    (`chainHash`, `firstBlockNum`/`numberOfBlocks`, `complete`,
    `shortChannelIds`, `timestamps`, `checksums`, `query_option`) are
    reproduced. Driven by `bolt7.zig`'s three "official BOLT#7 vector
    (lightning/bolts bolt07/extended-queries.json): ..." tests, which
    exercise both DECODE (parse the official `hex`, assert fields) and
    ENCODE (build from the vector's own decoded fields, assert byte-exact
    `hex`) for `query_channel_range`, `reply_channel_range`, and
    `query_short_channel_ids`.

    4 of the 10 rows use the `COMPRESSED_ZLIB` short_channel_id (and, in
    two of those, `query_flags`/`timestamps_tlv`) encoding — this module
    has no zlib codec (SPEC.md/README's Scope) and does not interpret
    `query_flags`' bit-array content at all regardless of its own
    encoding, so those rows' compressed *content* is not independently
    reconstructed; the module's codec treats the encoding-type byte plus
    payload as one opaque blob throughout, and `bolt7.zig`'s tests still
    check everything they express directly in the wire bytes (the
    encoding-type byte itself, `chain_hash`, the block-range/complete
    fields, and — since BOLT#7 never compresses it — `checksums_tlv`'s raw
    content even in an otherwise-`COMPRESSED_ZLIB` row) plus a byte-exact
    decode→re-encode round trip of the opaque blob. See `bolt7.zig`'s test
    bodies and `ANCHOR-TASKS.tsv`'s `lnwire` row for exactly which rows
    fall on which side.

## `lightningdevkit/rust-lightning` — an independent Lightning
## implementation's own encode/decode test hex

  https://github.com/lightningdevkit/rust-lightning
  Dual-licensed MIT / Apache-2.0: `LICENSE-MIT`, `LICENSE-APACHE`, and
  `LICENSE.md` at the repository root (all three present; this module takes
  the MIT option, matching the rest of zig-libs).
  `lightning/src/ln/msgs.rs`, as of the last commit touching that file on
  the `main` branch at fetch time, commit `8b1c7710959c67742b051a9cfccf75a373ee30f5`
  (`main` branch tip `676bcb144173d742d4c61663123945a62ecc7736` at fetch
  time, 2026-08-02).

BOLT#7's own spec repository (`lightning/bolts`, above) carries no test
vectors for `channel_announcement`/`node_announcement`/`channel_update`.
`lightningdevkit/rust-lightning` — a separate, independent implementation,
not the spec — has three `#[test]` functions (`encoding_channel_announcement`,
`encoding_node_announcement`, `encoding_channel_update`, each calling a
`do_encoding_*` helper with every boolean-parameter combination the test
actually drives) that build a message from explicit field values and assert
the serialized bytes equal a hex literal. What is vendored here is only that
FROZEN HEX OUTPUT and the field values that produce it — never their
encoder/decoder's own source, and never read for its own sake (see "What an
anchor is" in the task that added this: reading an independent
implementation's CODE and writing tests from it would just relocate their
reading of BOLT#7 into this module's head; only the hex their encoder
actually emitted, transcribed mechanically, is an anchor).

  * `lightning/src/ln/msgs.rs` (22 combinations across the three messages,
    ALL vendored: 4 for `channel_announcement`, 10 for `node_announcement`,
    8 for `channel_update` — exactly the parameter tuples each upstream
    `#[test]` function calls its `do_encoding_*` helper with, none
    synthesized) — transcribed mechanically into
    `src/bolt7_announcement_update_kat_vectors.zig`; each combination's full
    wire hex (rust-lightning's `target_value`, which — unlike this module's
    own `decode*`/`serialize*` pair — never includes BOLT#1's 2-byte `type`
    field; see that file's module doc comment for how the type is
    reattached before driving this module's codec) plus every field value
    that produces it. Driven by `bolt7.zig`'s three "official BOLT#7 vector
    (rust-lightning msgs.rs encoding_*): ..." tests, which exercise both
    DECODE (parse the official bytes, assert every field including the
    4-signature order in `channel_announcement` and the excess/trailing
    data BOLT#7 requires be preserved) and ENCODE (build from the vector's
    own fields, assert byte-exact output) — closing the "round-trip only"
    gap `ANCHOR-TASKS.tsv`'s `lnwire` row previously recorded for these
    three messages. All 22 vectors are fully usable in both directions —
    none exercise a `lnwire`-unimplemented encoding (unlike 4 of the
    `bolt07/extended-queries.json` rows above), since this module's
    `node_announcement.addresses` and `channel_announcement.features` are
    opaque byte slices regardless of address-descriptor kind or feature-bit
    meaning.

  * `lightning/src/ln/msgs.rs`, `encoding_init`/`encoding_error`/
    `encoding_warning`/`encoding_ping`/`encoding_pong` (fetched 2026-08-08,
    `main` branch commit `3e0c26973a66e945088c64793b4e35cfb01b9b78`) — two of
    upstream's five `init` sub-cases (the ones with no legacy-globalfeatures
    derivation, so every input byte is the literal field value), plus
    `error`/`warning`'s shared vector and `ping`/`pong`, transcribed
    mechanically into `src/bolt1_kat_vectors.zig`. Closes the gap
    `ANCHOR-TASKS.tsv`'s `lnwire` row previously mis-recorded as covered:
    "BOLT#1 App A/B" (BigSize/TLV) anchors the *primitives* `init`/`error`/
    `ping`/`pong` are built from, not those messages themselves, which had 0
    external vectors before this pass — self round-trip and hostile-
    truncation only. Driven by `bolt1_kat_test.zig`.

Implementation vs. test-data distinction: the BOLT#1/#2/#7 wire-format
CODEC in `bolt1.zig`/`bolt2.zig`/`bolt7.zig` is this module's own
clean-room work from the public spec text (`01-messaging.md` /
`02-peer-protocol.md` / `07-routing-gossip.md`) — that needs no
attribution per root `NOTICE` §0 (a public spec is not a copyrightable
work). What is reproduced here, and only here, is third-party TEST-CASE
DATA — the SPECIFICATION AUTHORS' OWN vectors (`lightning/bolts`) and an
INDEPENDENT IMPLEMENTATION'S frozen encoder output (`rust-lightning`) —
which is precisely why this notice exists and lives beside it rather than
in root `NOTICE` (per root `NOTICE` §1's policy: attribution for reproduced
third-party data travels with the module that owes it).
